WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
THE STATE OF GOOGLE:&nbsp;

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:23.400
Bing, the search engine that everyone knows&nbsp;
about but no one uses. Or at least, that’s what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.220
we all remember it as. Bing has actually been&nbsp;
making quite a bit of progress against Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:37.980
especially in the desktop market. At the end of&nbsp;
2018, Google had a marketshare of 91.46% while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:45.240
Bing had a marketshare of a mere 3.36%. But,&nbsp;
by the end of 2022, Google’s marketshare had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:53.940
fallen to 84.08% while Bing’s marketshare rose to&nbsp;
8.95%. In October, Bing’s marketshare almost even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:59.820
crossed 10%, and looking at the trend, it appears&nbsp;
that this will happen within the next couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:07.020
months. Now of course, Google is still by far&nbsp;
the most dominant player, but at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.360
we can’t just write off this trend as random&nbsp;
market fluctuations either given the massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.340
size of the desktop market. Over 5 billion people&nbsp;
around the world have access to the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.620
and even if we assume only half of these&nbsp;
people use computers to access the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:29.040
that’s 2.5 billion people. This means that Bing’s&nbsp;
rise in market shares correlates to a rise from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:37.020
84 million users to 224 million, also known&nbsp;
as an extra 140 million users. It’s not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.760
market share numbers that are suggesting this&nbsp;
either. Google themselves suggest that interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.680
around Google has fallen substantially&nbsp;
from their peak back in 2014. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:54.780
interest in Google is only about 50% of its peak&nbsp;
which brings us back to 2011 levels. What makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:01.200
this even scarier for Google is that Yahoo was at&nbsp;
this same level of interest just 10 years ago. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.660
if Google follows a similar path to Yahoo, they&nbsp;
may very well be at single digit numbers in 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:12.120
years, but that’s of course the worst case&nbsp;
scenario. It’s quite unlikely that Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.780
will end up like Yahoo anytime soon. But it does&nbsp;
look Google’s hayday was nearly 10 years ago at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:25.020
this point. And looking at the trend, it appears&nbsp;
that there’s just even more downside ahead. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.060
wait a minute, Google’s while Google’s marketshare&nbsp;
has been trending down, it has fallen anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:35.160
near 50%. So why are people ditching Google and&nbsp;
where’s all this internet traffic going instead?

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:42.060
ALTERNATIVE SEARCH ENGINES:
Well, starting off with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:48.240
most obvious culprit, we have competing search&nbsp;
engines and particularly Bing. 10 to 15 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.480
you could’ve made the argument that one&nbsp;
search engine is better than another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.880
but nowadays, they all perform to a&nbsp;
pretty high standard including Bing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.440
practicality is something that will rarely get&nbsp;
people to switch between search engines given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.840
that they’re all about the same. But, something&nbsp;
that is a pretty good incentive is money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.240
If you’re not familiar with Microsoft’s Rewards&nbsp;
program, Microsoft will literally pay you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:19.200
using Bing. This might seem odd at first, but&nbsp;
it actually makes sense. Microsoft makes money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.880
from showing you sponsored search results, and&nbsp;
they simply share some of this revenue with you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.400
as a thank you. For every search you make on&nbsp;
Bing, you’ll earn 5 points, and as a newbie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.680
this is capped at 50 points per day or 10&nbsp;
searches. This is just for starters though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.900
if you actually use Bing regularly, Microsoft&nbsp;
will boost your limit to 250 points per day or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.960
50 searches. Each point roughly correlates to 1&nbsp;
cent, so you could easily be earning hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:51.240
dollars every single year for just doing what&nbsp;
you’re already doing but with Bing. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:57.000
actually a bunch of hardcore Bing users who have&nbsp;
this down to a science. This one reddit user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.120
for example, estimates that he’s earning $8.24&nbsp;
per hour by just using Bing normally. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.480
searching on Bing won’t replace your job, but it&nbsp;
might get a you free pizza or Chipotle every month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.580
or so. And many have figured, if they’re willing&nbsp;
to let Google sell their data, they might as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:20.100
well let Microsoft sell their data and at least&nbsp;
get free food out of it. Speaking of data, some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.660
people still have hope that they can secure their&nbsp;
privacy online, and many of these people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.880
chosen to use DuckDuckGo over Google. If you’re&nbsp;
not familiar with DuckDuckGo, they’re a search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:35.340
engine who’s entire pitch is basically “we’re&nbsp;
anti Google”. Essentially, they don’t collect any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:41.400
data about you and they have no idea who you even&nbsp;
are. Basically imagine Chrome incognito if it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:47.220
actually incognito. Aside from not tracking your&nbsp;
search history, DuckDuckGo also tries to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:52.860
you to encrypted versions of websites whenever&nbsp;
possible, but it’s not all sunshines and rainbows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:58.620
At the end of the day, DuckDuckGo is a company&nbsp;
who needs to make money, and the way they make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:05.220
money is of course sponsored search results. It’s&nbsp;
simply that these ads aren’t targetted. We should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:11.700
also note that while DuckDuckGo themselves don’t&nbsp;
collect data, DuckDuckGo is powered by Bing or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:16.860
Microsoft. They have tried their best to block&nbsp;
as many Microsoft tracking scripts as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:23.400
but they’re not 100% successful. Nonetheless,&nbsp;
for many, this is magnitudes better than using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:29.100
Bing or Google directly. For the more cynical&nbsp;
ones, well they figured, if you can’t fight em,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.480
you might as well join em, and these are&nbsp;
the people that flocked over to Bing.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.980
SOCIAL MEDIA:&nbsp;

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:43.260
Alright, so a subset of the population has&nbsp;
shifted over to using Bing and DuckDuckGo due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:49.020
to their appeals of money and privacy. While this&nbsp;
has made a dent in Google’s marketshare, it’s by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:55.620
no means the biggest culprit. The biggest culprit&nbsp;
is actually social media, and it’s not just in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:01.560
few niches either, it’s across the board. Take&nbsp;
the how to space for example. 10 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:07.140
you may have Googled “how to know if you’ve been&nbsp;
ghosted” and read a WikiHow article about it. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:13.260
now, you can sit back, relax, and reflect on your&nbsp;
failed social life on YouTube. Jokes aside though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.760
it was actually difficult to find high quality&nbsp;
how to’s on YouTube for niche topics 10 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.980
ago. But, now, you can find detailed videos on&nbsp;
literally anything that you can think of. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:28.140
I don’t know about you, but for me, following&nbsp;
videos is way easier than following articles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.600
and pictures, not to mention more enjoyable.&nbsp;
The same thing could be said about news as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.440
well. 10 years ago, if you wanted to&nbsp;
follow the Fed or the stock market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.600
you’d probably have to read the New York&nbsp;
Times or Bloomberg or watch cable TV. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:48.300
now, there’s thousands of YouTube channels, TikTok&nbsp;
pages, Twitter accounts, Discord communities, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:53.280
WhatsApp groups that are relaying each and every&nbsp;
development faster and more digestible than CNBC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.760
This medium is just made even better by the fact&nbsp;
that it’s a lot more interactive. While NBC may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.740
have a massive viewerbase, you can’t just scroll&nbsp;
don’t to comments and share your thoughts or see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:10.200
what other people are thinking just like half of&nbsp;
you are probably doing right this very second. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:15.240
have virtually no way of reaching someone like&nbsp;
George Stephanopoulos or Jerome Powell. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.920
we can actually tweet at Elon Musk and there’s&nbsp;
actually a chance that he’ll reply and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.380
better chance that he’ll read it. Even if our&nbsp;
tweet doesn’t come across Elon, it’s more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.820
likely that hundreds of thousands if not millions&nbsp;
of people will see it especially if it’s something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:36.180
insightful or funny. But, social media making&nbsp;
everything more learnable, viewable, and enjoyable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:42.480
compared to Google is just half the story. The&nbsp;
other key point to note is that we no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:48.480
search for the content we consume. Statistically&nbsp;
speaking, 82.4% of you are here because YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.480
recommended this video to you. Only 5.2% of you&nbsp;
are here because you actually searched for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.340
And even amongst the people who are here from&nbsp;
search results, I bet you searched up Logically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:04.980
Answered as opposed to the actual title of this&nbsp;
video. This statistic is simply even more relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:09.960
for a platform like TikTok or Instagram where&nbsp;
you’re not really supposed to search at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:15.480
you’re just supposed to scroll. The algorithm&nbsp;
already knows what you wanna watch before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:21.360
know yourself whether it’s infotainment, funny&nbsp;
memes, or thirst traps. And when everything you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.640
need is offered to you without you even doing&nbsp;
anything, well you end up searching far less.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:31.500
AI:&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.840
While we’re all searching far less than we were&nbsp;
10 years ago, there’s still a place for searching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.840
Sometimes, you just need information fast. Maybe,&nbsp;
you need to fact check something or troubleshoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:48.540
something technical. In virtually all of these&nbsp;
cases, what you need isn’t fancy articles or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:55.440
videos, what you need is raw data. And any article&nbsp;
or video that you need to get through is simply an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:02.460
obstacle, not an enhancement. While this was often&nbsp;
annoying, for decades, this was something that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:07.800
simply didn’t even question. If you needed data&nbsp;
fast, you went to Google, searched for what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:13.020
want, scrolled past the sponsored results, clicked&nbsp;
on the most reputable website you could find, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:18.000
skimmed through until you found what you needed.&nbsp;
Oftentimes, you’d have to press the back button,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.400
go into another article, and do even more skimming&nbsp;
to truly find what you needed. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:29.220
this is a first world problem. 50 years&nbsp;
ago or even just without the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:34.680
getting information is literally a million&nbsp;
times harder. But, with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:40.560
we now have options that are way better than&nbsp;
even Google. ChatGPT, for example has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.300
making a lot of noise recently. You just have to&nbsp;
ask it a question, and it’ll do all the searching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:51.000
skimming, and compiling. But, ChatGPT&nbsp;
is just the latest example of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:56.220
One technology that has already been part of our&nbsp;
lives for years now is voice assistants like Siri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:02.760
Alexa, and Google. These assistants are already&nbsp;
great getting simple facts like how old is Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:07.800
Biden or what’s the weather like. For more&nbsp;
advanced questions, they often just direct you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:14.280
to Google.com, but that’s simply because they’re&nbsp;
not advanced enough yet. We should also note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:21.240
Apple doesn’t redirect you to Google.com out of&nbsp;
the kindness of their heart. Google actually pays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.680
Apple and Samsung an undisclosed amount every&nbsp;
year which is estimated to be in the billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.600
just to stay the default search engine. So,&nbsp;
you can bet that once AI becomes good enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:36.300
to reliably replace Google.com, these guys&nbsp;
are gonna be the first to jump ship. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:41.940
we should note that Google themselves will also&nbsp;
be jumping ship, so it’s not like Google as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:48.180
company will be left behind, but Google.com will.&nbsp;
This shift towards AI will simply be accelerated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.800
by the fact that these AI offerings will likely&nbsp;
be a lot better at telling you what you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.480
here. They’ll be able to cherry pick data points&nbsp;
and form arguments and answers that fit your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:03.120
perspectives and beliefs. This is one of the main&nbsp;
reasons social media platforms are so successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.400
in the first place. They’re able to serve you&nbsp;
content that you already agree with. While this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.780
creates massive echo chambers and is terrible&nbsp;
for having healthy dicussions and debates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:19.140
it’s perfect for maximizing user retention and&nbsp;
usage. And subconsciously, people will enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:25.520
using these platforms even more. And before you&nbsp;
know it, Google.com will be a relic from the past.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:30.780
THE FUTURE OF GOOGLE:
In the end, the search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:36.180
interest around Google has declined substantially&nbsp;
over the past several years. Some of this can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:41.520
attributed to direct competitors like Bing and&nbsp;
DuckDuckGo, but most of this actually has to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.080
with the rapidly shrinking search market.&nbsp;
While most of us probably didn’t notice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:52.020
on average, we don’t search nearly as much as&nbsp;
we once used to because we simply don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:57.720
to. We’re able to get all the information that&nbsp;
we need from social media and AI. None of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:02.640
is to say that Google as a company is screwed&nbsp;
by any means. First of all, they own YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:07.980
the largest social media platform in the world.&nbsp;
They’re also involved in thousands of businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:14.460
from maps and email to operating systems and the&nbsp;
cloud. Also, Google is at the forefront of this AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:19.980
revolution and are perfectly positioned for the&nbsp;
shift. So, Google as a whole will be just fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:25.320
but that’s why Google.com’s interest has been&nbsp;
falling off a cliff. Did you realize how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:30.960
less we tend to use search engines. Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you’re excited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:36.360
but also scared for AI. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:40.020
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

